$ 0 File | Load room...
This option allows you to load a screen saved with<1, File | Save room...>.
From the file selector, click on the screen you want to load.

Note that if the screen has been passworded then before you can load it you
have to enter the correct password using<41, Misc | Password >.
$ 1 File | Save room...
This allows you to save the current screen to disk.

To name your work, click on the yellow "*.CRM" and type in the new name.

See also: <0, File | Load room... >
$ 3 File | Load scene...
Loads a new picture in to be displayed during the game.

This is the first thing you'll do when creating a new room.
You can change the scene of a finished room by loading in a new scene but
then you'll have to do all the walls, etc again...

The scenes are VGA 320x200 or 640x400 256-colour PCX or BMP images. They can
be created using many paint programs.
$ 10 Walk-behind | View
This option draws all the walk-behind areas onto the screen in the correct
colours.

See also: <11, Walk-behind | Add >
$ 11 Walk-behind | Add
Creates a new walk-behind area in the current colour.

To draw the walk-behind area, draw each edge of it by starting the line
by clicking the LEFT button and finishing it with the RIGHT button.
Only finish the last edge by clicking the LEFT button. You then
have to click inside the shape and it will be filled in (so make sure all
the edges join up).

See also: <10, Walk-behind | View   >  <13, Walk-behind | Change >
          <12, Walk-behind | Remove >
$ 12 Walk-behind | Remove
Removes walk-behind areas created with <11, Walk-behind | Add >.

Use the same procedure as for adding, except that when you click to fill
the area will be removed.
$ 13 Walk-behind | Change
Cycles through the 15 available walk-behind colours.

Walk-behind colours are necessary because the main engine needs to know
what Y position the character needs to be above for him to be drawn behind
the walk-behind area. Each colour has a<14, base line, >which defines this.
$ 14 Walk-behind | Base
Changes the base line for the current colour.

The base line defines when the character will be drawn behind the walk-behind
area. If the character's feet are above the base line then he will be drawn
behind it, otherwise he will be drawn in front.
For this reason different colours are needed because not all walk behind
areas have the same base.

Use the LEFT button to position the line and the RIGHT button to confirm the
position.

See also: <13, Changing the colour >
$ 20 Walls | View
Draws all the walls on the screen.

This shows all the walls that have been added using<21, Walls | Add. >It is
necessary to use this command if the screen has been overwritten, for example
to view the<33, condition areas. >
$ 21 Walls | Add
Walls block the character's feet from walking over them. You place walls
along the bottom of a fence, around a boulder, etc.

To add a wall, click at the beginning and end. To make a curved wall, draw
lots of short straight walls next to each other.

You can press A instead of choosing  Walls | Add  to add walls.

See also: <20, Walls | View >  <22, Walls | Remove >
$ 22 Walls | Remove
Removes walls created with<21, Walls | Add. >

Just click on the beginning and end of a line and it will be removed.

After using this command the screen may not look right: just choose
<20,Walls | View >to sort it out.
$ 30 Condition | Change
This is where you define how the game<105, reacts >to different events.
Use the UP and DOWN arrows and PGUP and PGDN to scroll the list, and
press RETURN to change the reaction.

Also on this screen are another 3 columns, one headed "Score", one headed
"Data" and the other headed "Misc". The Score column is where you give the
player points when the condition occurs. The points are only given once, even
if the event occurs several times. The Data column is where you give
<105,extra information >about the reaction. With some reactions this is
necessary, with others it's optional. The Misc column is where you can set
another reaction to happen after the current one (chained).

Press S to change the score, D to change Data and M to change Misc.
$ 31 Condition | Edges
This is where you define the edges of the screen. These are used as the
boundaries the character must pass in order to create conditions 1-4.
They also define where the character will appear when it enters the screen.

Click the RIGHT button to cycle through the different edges, and press
the LEFT button to move the currently selected wall.

See also: <30, Changing conditions >
$ 32 Condition | View screen
Draws on the screen all the condition areas.

This is used to view where the condition areas are. It is necessary because
either the Walls + Walk-behind areas or the Condition areas can be displayed
at one time.
$ 33 Condition | Set screen
Allows you to add a condition area in the current colour.

You add condition areas the same way you add<11, walk-behind areas. >
$ 34 Condition | Remove
Removes previously added condition areas.

You remove condition areas the same way you remove<12, walk-behind areas. >
$ 35 Condition | Select
Cycles through the 15 available condition area colours.

Each colour can be set with a different reaction to events occuring on it.
$ 36 Condition | Messages
This screen is where you create and edit room messages.

To create a new message, press A. To choose the message you want to edit,
use the LEFT and RIGHT arrow keys. When you're on the correct message, press
RETURN to change it. Note that you must re-type the whole message.

To force a carriage return to be displayed in the game instead of the words
wrapping round, just type a bracket [ into the message.

You can use F2 to toggle whether the next message will be displayed after
this one. This allows you to have conversations and long descriptions.

F3 changes how the message will look when it is displayed. Normal means
in the standard grey box. Red and purple speech display the text in speech
marks at the top of the screen in the respective colours.
$ 40 Misc | Objects
This command lets you position objects onto the screen. Objects, unlike the
background, can be moved and taken.

Click the LEFT button to position the object, press N to change what it
looks like, and press A to cycle through the objects. To add an object, just
cycle through to the first unused number (0 is first position) and press
the LEFT button, which will automatically appear as a cup.
You can switch an object off by pressing the O key. This means it will not
appear on the screen to begin with, but you can switch it on with the <105,"turn
object on" response>. Note that moving an object (clicking the LEFT button)
will automatically turn it back on.

Once you're done, hit ESCAPE to return to the main screen.

NOTE: Don't create, for example, object 3 if you haven't created object 2. Go
      through the numbers in ascending order.

See also: <43, Misc | Sprite manager >
$ 41 Misc | Password
This screen is where you enter the password to be used for loading and
saving screens.

To password protect your screen, enter a password here by clicking in the
box and typing it in. Then save your work and the password will be saved.

To load a password protected screen, enter the password here by clicking in
the box, and then choose<0, File | Load room... >.

See also: <1, File | Save room... >
$ 42 Misc | Options
This allows you to change various screen-related options. At present
they are:

Start music    ³ Defines the music to play when the player enters the screen.
Can save/load  ³ Whether the save and load functions work on this screen.
Man visible    ³ Whether the main character appears on the this screen.

To return to the main interface click outside the window.
$ 43 Misc | Sprite Manager
The sprite manager is part of RoomMake which allows you to import your own
graphics for objects.

The screen shows a grid of the current 'sprite set' - that is, all the object
graphics in the entire game. To import a graphic of your own in BMP or PCX
format, select the graphic you want to overwrite by clicking on it and then
clicking on "import". If you don't want to overwrite anything, choose
"import new" instead which will create a new slot for the object.
The imported graphic will be displayed on the screen. You can press ENTER to
import the whole picture, or you can drag a rectangle with the mouse to only
import a portion of it.

When you are done, hit ESC to exit the sprite manager. You will be asked
whether you want to save the new sprite set.

See also: <40, Misc | Objects >
$ 44 Misc | View Manager
The view manager allows you to create and edit 'views'. The meaning of a view
is described in the documentation.

To change the graphic for a frame, double-click on the frame, then choose a
picture from the current sprite set.
To add a new frame to a loop, click on the grey rectangle at the far right
of the loop.
To remove a frame, right-click on it.

To remove an entire loop, right-click on its number at the left of the screen.
To add a new loop to a view, click on the button at the bottom of the screen.

To cycle through the views, click "prev view" or "next view" at the bottom of
the screen.
$ 45 Misc | Ask Position
This screen allows you to find out the X and Y co-ordinates of a point on
the screen. This is useful mainly for two things:
 - The Data column with the "Go to screen" response to specify an offset
 - A move-to command in an animation sequence.

Click the LEFT mouse button on the screen and the co-ordinates of the point
where you clicked are displayed in the status bar.

When you are done, hit any key to return to the Room Editor.
